\name{comparisons}
\alias{comparisons}
\alias{comparisons.default}
\alias{comparisons.cca}
\alias{print.vegan_comparisons}
\title{Comparisons for Constrained Ordination Models}
\description{
Construct comparisons of factor levels in a fitted constrained ordination
model and test their additional constrained inertia with permutation
pseudo-\eqn{F} statistics. Comparisons are defined from counterfactual
model-matrix rows, so interactions and other model terms are honoured.
}
\usage{
comparisons(object, ...)

\method{comparisons}{cca}(object, variables, comparison = "pairwise",
    reference = NULL, newdata = NULL, by = NULL, hypothesis = NULL,
    joint = FALSE, permutations = how(nperm = 999), strata = NULL,
    parallel = getOption("mc.cores"), adjust = "none", wts = NULL, ...)

\method{print}{vegan_comparisons}(x, ...)
}
\arguments{
\item{object}{A constrained ordination object fitted by the formula
interface, such as a result from \code{rda}, \code{cca}, or \code{dbrda}.}
\item{variables}{The name of one factor whose levels are compared.}
\item{comparison}{A built-in comparison name, character equations, a named
list, or a numeric contrast vector or matrix.}
\item{reference}{The reference factor level. The first level is used when
this is omitted.}
\item{newdata}{The population over which model-matrix rows are averaged.
The default uses the fitted analysis data. Character value \code{"balanced"}
constructs a reference grid, and a data frame supplies an explicit population.}
\item{by}{Optional model variables defining groups within which comparisons
are computed.}
\item{hypothesis}{An optional specification combining rows generated by
\code{comparison}. Positional names \code{b1}, \code{b2}, and so on refer
to those rows.}
\item{joint}{Test all resulting rows as one joint hypothesis.}
\item{permutations}{A permutation count, matrix, or control object returned
by \code{\link[permute]{how}}.}
\item{strata}{An optional factor or integer vector restricting permutations.}
\item{parallel}{Number of parallel workers or a predefined socket cluster.}
\item{adjust}{A multiple-testing method accepted by
\code{\link[stats]{p.adjust}}.}
\item{wts}{Optional non-negative averaging weights, or the name of a weight
column in \code{newdata}.}
\item{x}{A result returned by \code{comparisons}.}
\item{\dots}{Arguments passed to methods or to \code{print.data.frame}.}
}
\details{
Built-in specifications are \code{"pairwise"}, \code{"revpairwise"},
\code{"sequential"} (also \code{"consecutive"}),
\code{"revsequential"}, \code{"reference"}, \code{"revreference"},
\code{"trt.vs.ctrl"}, \code{"deviation"} (also \code{"effect"}),
\code{"helmert"}, and \code{"poly"}.

Character comparisons are linear equations in factor-level names, for
example \code{"HF - BF = 0"} or \code{"(NM + SF) / 2 - BF = 0"}.
Names containing special characters must be enclosed in backticks. Only
linear comparisons with zero as the null value are supported.

With \code{newdata = NULL}, the focal factor is set to every fitted level in
every analysis row. The resulting model matrices are averaged, leaving the
empirical distribution of other model variables intact. Thus interactions
are averaged over observed moderators. Use \code{by} or explicit
\code{newdata} for simple effects at particular moderator values.
When both \code{by} and \code{hypothesis} are supplied, positional
\code{b} names refer to all grouped comparison rows in their printed order.
This supports interaction contrasts such as differences between simple
effects in two moderator groups.
Because each \code{b} quantity is already a comparison, second-stage
hypothesis weights need not sum to zero; expressions such as
\code{"b1 + b2 - b3 = 0"} are supported.

Internally, the model space permitted under the null hypothesis is added to
original \code{Condition} terms, and the complementary tested space is
passed to vegan's reduced-model permutation machinery. Inertia and
pseudo-\eqn{F} use the geometry of the fitted method. Encoded coefficients
are not reported or tested individually.

See \code{vignette("comparisons-vegan")} for a user-oriented guide to the
complete interface and a technical appendix describing the tested space.
}
\value{
A data frame of class \code{"vegan_comparisons"} containing the comparison,
hypothesis degrees of freedom, inertia, proportion of total inertia,
pseudo-\eqn{F}, and permutation p-value. Grouping variables and adjusted
p-values are included when requested. Attributes retain compiled comparison
matrices and permutation metadata.
}
\examples{
data(dune)
data(dune.env)
mod <- rda(dune ~ Management + A1, data = dune.env)
comparisons(mod, "Management", permutations = 99)
comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "reference",
            reference = "BF", permutations = 99)
comparisons(mod, "Management",
            comparison = c("HF - BF = 0", "(NM + SF) / 2 - BF = 0"),
            permutations = 99)

imod <- rda(dune ~ Management * A1, data = dune.env)
comparisons(imod, "Management", newdata = data.frame(A1 = c(3, 6)),
            permutations = 99)

comparisons(mod, "Management", comparison = "pairwise",
            hypothesis = "b2 - b1 = 0", permutations = 99)
}
\seealso{\code{\link{anova.cca}}, \code{\link{rda}}, \code{\link{cca}},
\code{\link{dbrda}}, \code{\link[permute]{how}}}
\keyword{models}
\keyword{multivariate}
